<!-- ═════════ 01. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY ═════════ -->
<div class="container">
<details class="accordion">
<summary class="accordion-header">
<div class="accordion-num">01</div>
<div class="accordion-text">
<span class="accordion-title">Executive Summary</span>
<span class="accordion-subtitle">Strategic position, key findings, and three moves for Simplii</span>
</div>
<div class="accordion-icon">+</div>
</summary>
<div class="accordion-body">

<h3>Strategic position</h3>
<p>Simplii owns the no-fee, digital-first lane in Canadian retail banking at 20% of the sample, backed by three moat components. First, the shared CIBC ATM network: 58% of Simplii customers say it was a reason they chose Simplii over the alternatives. Second, the no-fee promise, delivered: 97% of Simplii customers cite the no-fee model as something their bank does well, and zero cite monthly fees as a current frustration. Third, e-transfer speed: 60% of Simplii customers say it is one of Simplii's strengths versus 14% of Big-5 customers who say it about their bank. The customer base is characterized by higher-income urban professionals who chose Simplii as a deliberate rational decision, not budget shoppers. 68% of them are contingent stayers and a further 32% are retention risk. Zero Simplii customers meet the strict stable-loyalist definition. Growth is constrained almost entirely by non-customer mental availability: Simplii's own customers fully associate the brand with everyday spending, but only 35% of non-customers do. Closing that gap is the single highest-ROI lever the data identifies.</p>

<h3>Key findings</h3>

<div class="insight">
<p><span class:list={["tier-pill", tierPill(execClaims?.cep1_gap?.tier || 'strong').cls]}>{tierPill(execClaims?.cep1_gap?.tier || 'strong').label}</span><strong>1. Simplii's single largest growth lever is non-customer mental availability for everyday spending and bill payments.</strong> 100% of Simplii customers asked about this Category Entry Point (CEP) name Simplii; only 35% of non-customers do. That 65 percentage-point buyer-to-non-buyer gap is the largest of any bank-by-CEP combination in the dataset. Simplii's own customers fully credit it for everyday banking; non-customers simply do not make the association. This is the highest-ROI brand and marketing investment in the report.</p>
<span class="owner">Owner: Brand &amp; Marketing &middot; p&lt;0.001, subgroup vs all others (α=0.10 two-tailed)</span>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><span class:list={["tier-pill", tierPill(execClaims?.cibc_source?.tier || 'strong').cls]}>{tierPill(execClaims?.cibc_source?.tier || 'strong').label}</span><strong>2. CIBC is Simplii's largest source of recent switchers in our sample at 32% of Simplii customers.</strong> TD is second at 23%; Scotiabank is the smallest at 10%. CIBC defectors who chose Simplii are the fee-frustrated segment (100% cite monthly fees as a push, 95% cite fee-value mismatch). CIBC defectors who went to other Big-5 banks left for advisor, mortgage, or service reasons. Simplii is functionally CIBC's controlled-release valve for fee-driven defection, not a cross-category competitor. This shapes positioning and acquisition priority order.</p>
<span class="owner">Owner: Retail &amp; Growth and Brand &amp; Marketing &middot; p={execClaims?.cibc_source?.p?.toFixed(3)}, corroborated by fee-frustration profile specificity</span>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><span class:list={["tier-pill", tierPill(execClaims?.higher_income?.tier || 'strong').cls]}>{tierPill(execClaims?.higher_income?.tier || 'strong').label}</span><strong>3. Among recent switchers in BC and Alberta age 30 to 60, Simplii's customers skew higher-income, urban, and long-tenured at their previous bank.</strong> 30% earn $150K to $200K, 10 percentage points higher than the rest of the sample. Only 2% earn under $50K, 7 points lower. 60% are urban. 52% switched after a relationship of 10 or more years at their previous bank. These are deliberate, high-commitment switchers, not budget shoppers. This profile reflects switchers in this geography and age range and may not represent Simplii's full customer base, which we did not survey.</p>
<span class="owner">Owner: Brand &amp; Marketing &middot; income overrepresentation p={execClaims?.higher_income?.p?.toFixed(3)} (corroborated by urban skew and long-tenure pattern)</span>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><span class:list={["tier-pill", tierPill(execClaims?.structural_three?.tier || 'strong').cls]}>{tierPill(execClaims?.structural_three?.tier || 'strong').label}</span><strong>4. Simplii's three dominant current frustrations are structural trade-offs of the no-fee model, not execution failures.</strong> Limited product breadth at 73% of Simplii customers (72 points above all others), no physical branch access at 68% (68 points above), unwanted fragmentation at 53% (47 points above). 78 to 82% of the customers who cite these are still satisfied with Simplii overall, indicating they accept the trade-off. The mental-availability data corroborates: zero mentions on mortgage, retirement, financial advice, and small-business banking from anyone, including Simplii's own customers. Manage and reframe these. Do not try to build them.</p>
<span class="owner">Owner: Product &amp; Digital &middot; all three frustrations p&lt;0.001 (subgroup vs all others)</span>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><span class:list={["tier-pill", tierPill(execClaims?.zero_loyalists?.tier || 'strong').cls]}>{tierPill(execClaims?.zero_loyalists?.tier || 'strong').label}</span><strong>5. Simplii is the only bank in the study with zero stable loyalists under a strict definition</strong> (high satisfaction AND no more than one current frustration). The five other banks each have at least one. TD has the highest share at 15% of its customers. CIBC, RBC, BMO, and Scotiabank fall between 2% and 10%. 68% of Simplii's customers are contingent stayers and 32% are retention risk. This is a structural feature of the Simplii model, not a retention execution problem, and it should shape retention strategy from the ground up.</p>
<span class="owner">Owner: Retail &amp; Growth &middot; Fisher's exact p={execClaims?.zero_loyalists?.p?.toFixed(3)} (corroborated by structural trade-off pattern)</span>
</div>

<div class="insight">
<p><span class:list={["tier-pill", tierPill(execClaims?.cibc_regression?.tier || 'hedge').cls]}>{tierPill(execClaims?.cibc_regression?.tier || 'hedge').label}</span><strong>6. Directionally, phone wait times and app quality may regress for CIBC-to-Simplii migrants, a pattern worth monitoring.</strong> Among the 19 Simplii customers who came from CIBC, 32% cited phone waits as a reason for leaving CIBC and 42% now cite phone waits as a current Simplii frustration (a 17% regret rate). App quality shows a parallel but smaller shift (37% named it as a push force, 21% still cite it at Simplii, 29% regret rate). The subgroup-level test against other Simplii origins is not significant (p={execClaims?.cibc_regression?.p?.toFixed(2)}) because the cohort is small at n=19 and Simplii's overall phone-frustration baseline is close to the CIBC-origin rate. The pattern is suggestive but not conclusive. Corroborating signal: app quality regresses in the same cohort, same direction, independent theme. Treat this as a flag for Product &amp; Digital to watch, not a directive recommendation on its own.</p>
<span class="owner">Owner: Product &amp; Digital &middot; p={execClaims?.cibc_regression?.p?.toFixed(2)} (hedge: directional, corroborated by in-cohort app-quality regression)</span>
</div>

<h3>Three moves for Simplii</h3>

<table>
<thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Move</th><th style="width:50%">Description</th><th style="width:30%">Data anchor</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>1. Close the everyday-spending non-buyer gap</strong></td>
<td>Concentrate brand-building investment on the everyday-spending Category Entry Point among non-customers. Distinctive brand assets, category-entry-point advertising, and repeated linkage of "Simplii equals everyday banking" for the 65% of non-customers who do not currently make the association.</td>
<td>Mental-availability battery on everyday spending and bill payments: Simplii customer recall 100%, non-customer recall 35%, a 65 percentage-point lift, the largest in the dataset.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>2. Investigate service ops for the CIBC-origin cohort</strong></td>
<td><em>Directional, not yet conclusive.</em> Instrument the CIBC-origin service experience (phone wait times, app issue rates, first-90-day friction tickets) and validate against operational data before committing to a service-ops investment thesis. Two push-to-current regressions in the same 19-person cohort (phone waits and app quality, same direction, independent themes) are a signal worth chasing, but the subgroup test is not significant at n=19. Validate, then invest.</td>
<td>CIBC-to-Simplii cohort (n=19): of those who left CIBC citing phone waits, 42% still cite phone waits as a current Simplii frustration (32% push to 42% current, 17% regret). App quality shows the same in-cohort pattern (37% push to 21% current, 29% regret). p=0.55 on phone subgroup-vs-others test; tier: hedge.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>3. Reframe the structural three; do not build</strong></td>
<td>Limited product breadth, no branch access, and unwanted fragmentation are structural consequences of the no-fee model. Publish a partner playbook (investing, mortgage, advice) positioning fragmentation as user choice, not a Simplii gap. Do not advertise presence in mortgage shopping, retirement and investing, financial advice for life events, or small-business banking; the brand has zero mental availability in any of these among its own customers and among non-customers.</td>
<td>Current-frustration prevalence among Simplii customers: limited product breadth 73%, no physical branch access 68%, unwanted fragmentation 53%; 78 to 82% of the customers who cite these are still satisfied with the bank overall. Mental availability on mortgage shopping, retirement and investing, financial advice for life events, and small-business banking: zero mentions from anyone, including own customers.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
