<p><strong>What the intensity scoring means.</strong> Each of the two coders rated every (participant, theme) pair on a 1-to-3 intensity scale based on how the participant talked about the issue. <strong>Low intensity (1)</strong> = a brief mention without elaboration ("the fees were high"). <strong>Moderate intensity (2)</strong> = the participant elaborated with reasoning or specifics ("the monthly fee was $14.95 and I never used a teller"). <strong>High intensity (3)</strong> = the participant told a full story with emotion or a decisive moment ("I sat down with my partner one Sunday, added up what we'd paid in fees over a year, and that was the day I decided to leave"). High-intensity counts are the behaviorally significant stories. Use them to size trigger audiences, not the raw mention rate.</p>
