<!-- ═════════ 03. COMPETITIVE POSITION ═════════ -->
<div class="container">
<details class="accordion">
<summary class="accordion-header">
<div class="accordion-num">03</div>
<div class="accordion-text">
<span class="accordion-title">Competitive Position Analysis</span>
<span class="accordion-subtitle">Brand mental availability, migration flows, per-competitor signatures, moat assessment</span>
</div>
<div class="accordion-icon">+</div>
</summary>
<div class="accordion-body">

<p>Competitive position is the Brand &amp; Marketing foundation. Four lenses: mental availability for each Category Entry Point (CEP, the situation a buyer is in when a brand should come to mind), where customers come from, what distinguishes each competitor's frustration fingerprint, and a Moat Assessment for Simplii.</p>

<h3>3a. Brand mental availability by Category Entry Point</h3>
<p>For each Category Entry Point (CEP), we asked half the sample which financial institutions come to mind. Set A (n=150) was asked about everyday spending, retirement and investing, and financial advice for life events. Set B (n=150) was asked about digital banking, mortgage shopping, and small-business banking. The split-sample design keeps each CEP fresh in the participant's mind without conditioning answers across CEPs.</p>
<p>For each bank-by-CEP cell, we report the percentage of <em>current customers</em> of that bank who name it for the CEP, and the percentage of <em>non-customers</em> who name it. Cells are sorted by non-customer recall (the metric that matters for growth). Stars in the non-customer column flag banks whose non-customer recall is meaningfully above the average across the other banks for that CEP.</p>

