<p>Simplii's Brand &amp; Marketing agenda is dominated by one lever: close the everyday-spending non-customer gap. Everything else is either how to do that or how to avoid undermining it.</p>

<h3>4a. Who to target (Ideal Customer Profile)</h3>

<div class="criterion-block">
<h4>Income</h4>
<p class="criterion-signal">Among Simplii customers in the sample, 30% earn $150K to $200K (10 percentage points above the rest of the sample) and only 2% earn under $50K (7 points below).</p>
<div class="opp-callout"><p><strong>Targeting implication:</strong> mid-six-figure urban professionals, not price-constrained segments. Avoid "save money" framing as the lead.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="criterion-block">
<h4>Geography (Urban / Suburban / Rural)</h4>
<p class="criterion-signal">60% urban (6 points above), 12% rural (7 points below).</p>
<div class="opp-callout"><p><strong>Targeting implication:</strong> urban-skewing media and placements. Rural campaigns are a poor product fit because there is no branch to anchor the relationship.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="criterion-block">
<h4>Life stage</h4>
<p class="criterion-signal">71% of Simplii customers in the sample are aged 30 to 50, with a small skew toward 30 to 40.</p>
<div class="opp-callout"><p><strong>Targeting implication:</strong> mid-career, early-family. Life-event triggers (home purchase, family formation, job change) drove 58% of Simplii switches and convert at narrative intensity.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="criterion-block">
<h4>Previous-bank tenure</h4>
<p class="criterion-signal">52% of Simplii's switchers came from a relationship of 10 or more years at their previous bank.</p>
<div class="opp-callout"><p><strong>Targeting implication:</strong> do not chase casual shoppers. Target long-tenured customers who have accumulated reasons to leave. The slow-burn of accumulated frustrations was the push for 50% of Simplii switchers; nurture-sequence work beats one-shot promos.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="criterion-block">
<h4>Source bank</h4>
<p class="criterion-signal">CIBC contributes 32% of Simplii's switchers, TD 23%; together more than half.</p>
<div class="opp-callout"><p><strong>Targeting implication:</strong> concentrated source-bank targeting beats broad-audience campaigns. See Section 06 Competitive Acquisition Playbooks.</p></div>
</div>

<h3>4b. Positioning recommendation</h3>
<p>Lead with the no-fee model as a deliberate rational choice. The demographic profile (higher-income urban professionals, long-tenured switchers) supports "smart people who refuse to pay monthly fees they can avoid," not "save money" as a budget appeal. The CIBC ATM network is a moat component that reduces perceived friction; it is not the headline.</p>

<div class="vuln-callout">
<p><strong>Messaging that backfires.</strong></p>
<p>1. Do not claim "complete banking" or "all-in-one." 73% of Simplii customers cite limited product breadth as a current frustration. Zero mental availability on mortgage, retirement, advice, and small-business corroborates that the gaps are structural and known. Over-promising breadth produces credibility backlash.</p>
<p>2. Do not lead with CIBC ownership as the hero asset. 32% of Simplii customers specifically came from CIBC because Simplii is not CIBC. The ATM access is reassurance, not the headline.</p>
<p>3. Do not position as budget banking. Only 2% of Simplii customers earn under $50K. The lane is deliberate-choice, not budget-constrained.</p>
<p>4. Do not lead on savings rate. Savings rate is a current vulnerability (35% cite as a frustration; 58% regret rate among those who originally pushed on it). Promising a rate advantage Simplii does not consistently deliver converts acquisition into retention risk.</p>
</div>

