<h3>4f. CEP-based content pillars</h3>
<p>Three pillars: one lead, one secondary, and explicit non-pillars.</p>

<div class="opp-callout">
<p><strong>Lead pillar: own everyday spending and bill payments among non-customers.</strong> The 65 percentage-point buyer-to-non-buyer gap is the largest in the dataset. The target audience is the 65% of non-customers who do not currently associate Simplii with everyday banking. Tactics: category-entry-point advertising, distinctive brand assets (color, voice, sonic logo if one exists or can be built), repeated linkage of "Simplii equals everyday banking." This is a multi-year mental-availability build, not a campaign.</p>
</div>

<div class="opp-callout">
<p><strong>Secondary pillar: defend digital banking and mobile access.</strong> Simplii has a 17 percentage-point lead among customers vs non-customers, a closer fight. Tangerine and EQ Bank are the primary rivals. Tactics: reinforce Simplii's digital-banking credentials against pure-play digital challengers; protect e-transfer speed and app-parity messaging.</p>
</div>

<div class="vuln-callout">
<p><strong>Explicit non-pillar: do not attempt to build presence in mortgage shopping, retirement and investing, financial advice for life events, or small-business banking.</strong> Zero mentions on these CEPs from anyone, including Simplii's own customers. The product does not serve these categories. Advertising presence Simplii cannot deliver produces the limited-product-breadth frustration at market scale, confusing the brand positioning and damaging credibility. Refer customers to partner products (see Section 05, Wallet Fragmentation table).</p>
</div>

