<!-- ═════════ 08. METHODOLOGY ═════════ -->
<div class="container">
<details class="accordion">
<summary class="accordion-header">
<div class="accordion-num">08</div>
<div class="accordion-text">
<span class="accordion-title">Methodology</span>
<span class="accordion-subtitle">Sample design, statistical methodology, coding, known gaps, analytical approach</span>
</div>
<div class="accordion-icon">+</div>
</summary>
<div class="accordion-body">

<h3>8a. Sample design</h3>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">300</div><div class="stat-label">Participants</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">60/60/60/60/30/30</div><div class="stat-label">CIBC / Simplii / RBC / TD / BMO / Scotiabank</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">BC + AB</div><div class="stat-label">Geography</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">150 / 150</div><div class="stat-label">CEP Set A / Set B split-sample</div></div>
</div>
<p>All participants were Canadian adults age 30 to 60 in British Columbia or Alberta who had switched their primary personal checking bank within the past 24 months. Quota-balanced to oversample the four highest-priority banks (CIBC, Simplii, RBC, TD) relative to BMO and Scotiabank. 15-question interview guide covering brand mental availability (Q2 CEP battery), previous-bank push forces (Q5), evaluation process (Q6 to Q8), current-bank experience (Q10 and Q11), wallet composition (Q12 and Q13), forward-looking propensity (Q14), and macro sentiment (Q15). Split-sample CEP battery: Category Entry Points 1 (everyday spending), 4 (retirement and investing), 5 (financial advice for life events) asked of Set A (n=150); CEPs 2 (digital banking), 3 (mortgage shopping), 6 (small-business banking) asked of Set B (n=150). Split-sample integrity verified; no cross-contamination between sets.</p>

<h3>8b. Statistical methodology</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Alpha:</strong> 0.10 two-tailed. Interview studies at n=150 to 300 with subgroups that drop to 25 to 50 require the 0.10 threshold to surface commercial-decision signal. Market-research convention at this sample size.</li>
<li><strong>Default test:</strong> two-proportion z-test when every expected cell count in the 2x2 is at least 5. <strong>Fallback:</strong> Fisher's exact test when any expected cell count is below 5.</li>
<li><strong>Benchmark convention:</strong> bar charts with a dashed overall-average line display the overall sample average (including the subgroup), but the test compares the focal subgroup against all OTHER respondents combined. Every sig-flagged chart carries the required disclosure.</li>
<li><strong>Editorial discipline (three-tier rule):</strong> <em>Strong</em> = significant, recommendation language permitted; <em>Hedge</em> = non-significant but corroborated by an independent signal, "directionally" or "suggestive" language only; <em>No-claim</em> = non-significant and uncorroborated, not highlighted at all. Finding 6 in the Executive Summary is the one hedge in this report; every other exec finding is tier-1 strong.</li>
<li><strong>Competitive Messaging Matrix integrity:</strong> per-cohort join check {cmmCheck?.passed ? 'passed (no theme has identical prevalence across all six competitors)' : 'FAILED, REVIEW BEFORE PUBLISHING'} ({cmmCheck?.identical_rows?.length || 0} offenders). Filter-then-aggregate confirmed.</li>
<li><strong>Helper code:</strong> <code>research-projects/6 Analyzing and building a report/shared/significance_tests.py</code> and <code>analysis_helpers.py</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>8c. Coding and reliability</h3>
<div class="stat-grid">
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">83</div><div class="stat-label">Thematic codes</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0.929</div><div class="stat-label">Thematic Kappa<br/>(Almost Perfect)</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0.970</div><div class="stat-label">Direction Kappa</div></div>
<div class="stat-card"><div class="stat-num">0.472</div><div class="stat-label">Intensity Kappa</div></div>
</div>
<p>Dual independent Sonnet 4 coders with Opus 4 arbiter on disagreements. Each meaning unit received a thematic code plus a direction tag (positive, negative, or neutral). Each (participant, code) pair also received a 1-to-3 intensity score. Intensity Kappa fell below the threshold for headline use due to marginal skew (most scores at level 2 or 3); the BuyerVoice framework allows intensity to be reported as a coloring layer on top of the binary mention rate, with the binary rate as the primary signal.</p>

<h3>8d. Known study-design gaps</h3>
<div class="caveat">
<ul>
<li><strong>Sample is recent switchers age 30 to 60 in BC and Alberta only.</strong> Findings characterize this cohort, not the full Simplii customer base.</li>
<li><strong>Q11 fee_creep_over_time = 0 everywhere.</strong> Genuine, not a coding bug. All participants switched within 24 months and have not been at the new bank long enough to experience fee creep.</li>
<li><strong>CEP matrix coverage is six-bank-only.</strong> Institutions like Tangerine, EQ Bank, Wealthsimple, ATB Financial, and Vancity (named elsewhere in participant responses) are not in the matrix. This constrains the Simplii-vs-Tangerine mental-availability comparison to qualitative treatment in 3e.</li>
<li><strong>No unprompted CEP discovery.</strong> The B2C framework's open-ended "when does banking come to mind?" question was not included. Section 03 uses the prompted six-CEP battery only.</li>
<li><strong>No absolute brand rejection.</strong> The framework's "banks you'd never consider" question was removed. Section 04's Competitive Messaging Matrix uses Q7 evaluation-rejection (reasons participants who actively considered an alternative chose not to pick it), arguably richer behavioral data than absolute rejection but a different interpretation.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h3>8e. Analytical approach</h3>
<p>The report includes six analyses beyond the interview instrument's direct question-level aggregates:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Buyer vs non-buyer lift per CEP per bank</strong> (Byron Sharp / Ehrenberg-Bass mental-availability framework). Largest gap in the dataset is the highest-ROI brand-building lever.</li>
<li><strong>Migration Flow Matrix.</strong> Cross-tab of previous bank by current bank. Reveals CIBC to Simplii as the dominant flow.</li>
<li><strong>Contingent Stayer Segmentation.</strong> Three-tier classification using the propensity question, current-frustration count, and wallet-elsewhere indicator.</li>
<li><strong>Push-Pull Alignment</strong>, overall and per source bank. For each theme in both the push-force question and the current-frustration question, computes push prevalence, current prevalence, and regret rate among pushers. Verdict: resolved, partial, or regressed.</li>
<li><strong>Structural Trade-off Acceptance.</strong> Among customers who cite each structural frustration, what fraction still report overall satisfaction. Separates "fix it" from "reframe it."</li>
<li><strong>Per-competitor Competitive Messaging Matrix.</strong> Joined Q7 rejector cohort by Q7 rejection themes, per competitor. Produces the messaging fingerprint for each competing bank rather than reusing market-level aggregates.</li>
</ul>

</div>
